# GUI test protocol

How an outer agent gets one agentTools test of Controllers to pass on one desk.
<Test> is the test class; <desk> is the name of the desk in the on("<desk>",...) entries of the tests; <workspace> is the fearlessBranch folder of the outer agent (for example /data/fearlessBranch1).
The protocol is the same for this machine (ubuntu_gnome) and for a VM: only opening and closing differ.
The outer agent runs Pre, starts the inner agent with the prompt below, runs Post, and always ends with Finally, whatever happened before.

## Pre (outer agent)

1. Run the align-branches skill in <workspace>.
2. If <desk> is a VM: `/data/AgentsCoordination/linuxVersion/autoScripts/vm.sh open <desk>`
3. Run the recorded values, from <workspace>/Coordinator/Build/src:
   `/opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea --module-path ../../../Commons/Commons.jar --add-modules Commons scripts/TestAgentTools.java <desk> false <Test> /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> /data/pilotio`
   If it passes, go to Finally.
   This run is the cheapest way to know whether the recorded values work, so it always runs: when <desk> has no entries in <Test> it fails at once, after the build, because an action has no <desk> entry; that is the expected result, and Pre goes on.
4. In <workspace>/Controllers/test/agentTools, set every val(...) of the <desk> entries that <Test> uses (in <Test>.java and ManagerTest.java) to val(null).
   Give every other ManagerTest action with no <desk> entry one with the steps of its ubuntu_gnome entry and val(null).
5. `mkdir -p /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> /data/pilotio/shots_<desk>`
6. Start the test in agent mode as a background command, from <workspace>/Coordinator/Build/src:
   `/opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea --module-path ../../../Commons/Commons.jar --add-modules Commons scripts/TestAgentTools.java <desk> true <Test> /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> /data/pilotio > /data/pilotio/run_<desk>.txt 2>&1`

## Inner agent

Haiku 5.5, low effort, with the prompt below, where the outer agent writes <Test>, <desk> and <workspace> out.
The list of mistakes at its end holds what earlier runs did wrong; the outer agent adds a line when a run shows a new one.

```
You are the person at the desk in the agentTools test <Test> (<workspace>/Controllers/test/agentTools/<Test>.java; shared actions in ManagerTest.java), on the desk <desk>.
The screen of this machine shows that desk, ready. The test is already running and waits for you.

At each action the test creates an empty file named after the action in /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> and waits.
The on("<desk>",...) entry of that action says what to answer: each val(null) is one value, in order.
Answer by writing the values into that file, one per line, then a line ;
- on("<desk>",()->click(val(null),val(null))) for action managerMenu: `printf '412\n88\n;\n' > /data/pilotio/channel_<desk>/managerMenu`
- on("<desk>",()->waitAfter(val(null))) for action appsShown, milliseconds after the end of the previous action: `printf '100\n;\n' > /data/pilotio/channel_<desk>/appsShown`
- on("<desk>",()->keys(KeyEvent.VK_ENTER)) for action closeDialog, no value: `printf ';\n' > /data/pilotio/channel_<desk>/closeDialog`
- a text value is quoted: "hello"
Which action waits: `ls /data/pilotio/channel_<desk>`
The screen: `/opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea -cp <workspace>/out/modular/controller-test:<workspace>/Commons/Commons.jar agentTools.Pilot seen /data/pilotio/shots_<desk>/<n>.png`, then read the png; a purple ring marks the pointer.
The test output: /data/pilotio/run_<desk>.txt; when the test ends it shows how many tests succeeded and failed.
A wait value is for later runs without you: in this run you are slower than any wait, so every wait is already over when the test reads it, and that is intended.
A wait is 100 unless you expect the step to take longer.
What the manager should do: <workspace>/ZeroToHero/tasks/ControllersDesign. Your role: "Testing GUIs" in CLAUDE.md.

A failure has one of three causes:
(1) a value you gave was wrong, for example the pointer missed a target the screen shows;
(2) the test checks behaviour the design does not describe;
(3) a step of the test is not what a person does on this desk, or needs an adjustment for it.
A step that does nothing on this desk is (3), even if the test passes.
Stop at the first (2) or (3). Do not fix the test.
Change no file except the files of /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> and /data/pilotio/shots_<desk>.

Mistakes made in earlier runs of this prompt:
- a click answered on a step that did nothing on the desk (a click on an empty corner), called harmless instead of reported as (3);
- a value answered without looking at the screen first.

Report: for each action, the values you gave, what the screen showed, and why (for a wait above 100, why longer); how the test ended; for a failure, its cause 1, 2 or 3 with the evidence, and for (1) the better values.
After a (1), the outer agent starts the test again and sends you a message: answer the new run.
```

## Post (outer agent)

1. Report (1): stop the test (Finally 1), Pre 6 again, then message the inner agent `The test started again.`
2. Report (2) or (3): `git -C <workspace>/Controllers checkout test/agentTools`, report the finding to the user, and go to Finally.
3. Passed: write the values of the last run into the <desk> entries, then run Pre 3.
4. If Pre 3 fails: from the failing check and `agentTools.Pilot seen`, judge which earlier waits may be too short (not every action is followed by a check that verifies it), raise them with informed guesses, and run Pre 3 again.
   A wait above 500 gets a comment saying why. A failure that time does not explain goes to the user, with the test unchanged.
5. A passing <desk> entry set goes into the open Controllers PR of recorded entry sets, if one is open (`gh pr view <n> --repo FearlessLang/Controllers --json state`); otherwise into a new one.

## Finally (outer agent, always)

1. Stop the test, if it still runs (in agent mode it waits for its channel file forever):
   `ps -C java -o pid=,args= | awk '/TestAgentTools.java <desk> |-Ddesk=<desk> /{print $1}' | xargs -r kill`
2. If <desk> is a VM: `/data/AgentsCoordination/linuxVersion/autoScripts/vm.sh close <desk>`
3. `rm -rf /data/pilotio/channel_<desk> /data/pilotio/shots_<desk> /data/pilotio/run_<desk>.txt`
4. Check the desk is bare: `pgrep -af 'fearlessManaged|nautilus|gnome-terminal|ptyxis'` prints nothing.
